retired senate majority leader harry  reid has died at 82 after a fight with  pancreatic cancer for more than three  decades a nevada democrat was known as  one of the toughest deal makers in  congress during a time of immense  partisan divisions in a statement  president biden who served in the senate  with reid for 22 years said for harry it  wasn't about power for power's sake it  was about the power to do right for the  people  chief washington correspondent major  garrett has more on the legacy of the  longtime democratic leader  i didn't make it  in life because my athletic prowess  i didn't make it because of my good  looks  i didn't make it because i'm a genius  i made it because i worked hard those  words spoken to be the key to success by  a man who embodied what hard work and  tenacity truly means harry reid earned a  reputation as one of the toughest  lawmakers in congress the deal doesn't  have to be a fight  there's a simple path forward just put  all four bills together bring him the  floor a former amateur boxer he brought  a willingness to draw partisan blood and  famously carried the motto i would  rather dance than fight but i know how  to fight recoverings were willing to do  anything to take health care away from  millions of people  scores of votes to repeal it what a  waste of the taxpayers time reid grew up  in the small town of searchlight nevada  in a cabin with no indoor plumbing he  had to hitchhike 40 miles to attend high  school where he met his wife landry  gould she's been the being of my  existence  in my personal life and my public life  reed was elected to the house in 1982  rising to the heights of power serving  as senate majority leader in 2007  over his 34-year congressional career  read was behind many landmark democratic  victories pushing through a sweeping  economic stimulus after the great  recession and reed played a significant  role in passing the affordable care act  which he later described as his favorite  but hardest fight in the senate i want  people to stay nevada to know that i am  so grateful and i have done my best i  haven't been perfect but i've really  tried my hardest  garage joins us now along with cbs news  political contributor joel payne a  democratic party strategist who worked  for senator reid good morning to you  both major let's begin with you what do  you think his legacy will be  so let's start in nevada no one raises  rises to national prominence without  getting re-elected so in nevada harry  reid's legacy is environmental first and  foremost he kept something from  happening that was going to happen  making yucca mountain the final resting  place for all nuclear waste in this  country harry reid blocked that a  significant victory for nevada he also  brought the nevada presidential caucuses  to that state in 2008 and ever since  it's been third in line in the  democratic nominating process elevating  nevada's importance  also he built the most important  political machine in the state history  for 20 years or more anyone who made a  political move in nevada democrat or  republican had to vector off harry reid  and his political machine nationally he  was mitch mcconnell before mitch  mcconnell what do i mean by that he was  a tenacious party and senate leader who  used all the procedural rules of that  body to get what he wanted to achieve  often to the consternation and boiling  fury of his political opposition he also  centralized power in leadership offices  in ways even lyndon johnson did not  lastly on a national level he  intensified what was already happening  an intense fight over the future of the  federal bench that legacy on the federal  bench and its implications for the  supreme court also part of harry reid's  legacy  joel speaking of  the current minority leader mitch  mcconnell he praised senator reid on  twitter yesterday saying even though  they had intense conflict he appreciated  the friendship having worked for him  what was that relationship really like  in the halls of congress and what stands  out to you about how he conducted  himself on the hill  well harry reid was an amateur boxer and  styles make fights and i guess you could  say reed mcconnell probably looked at  each other and saw similar fighters they  saw mirror images of each other people  who were you know for lack of a better  term gym rats who worked their way up  from the back bench of the senate all  the way up to leadership and just  understand how to get hard things done i  think that's the read legacy major  talked a little bit about  some of those accomplishments nationally  i worked for him at the beginning of the  obama administration in 2009 and 10. and  i'll tell you it was the most productive  congress in history you just go back and  look at those accomplishments the aca  but wall street reform the lilly  ledbetter fair pay act uh for for  fairness for women in the workplace uh  you know i mean the repeal of don't ask  don't tell  s chip 911 first responders bill it was  a modern day great society and harry  reid helped lord over it  major speaking of former president obama  he encouraged then senator obama to run  for president president obama said in  the statement last night i wouldn't have  been president had it not been for your  encouragement and support i wouldn't  have gotten i would wouldn't have got  most of what i'd got done without your  skill and determination  let's talk about that skill and  determination and some of the  legislation that was passed major  including the affordable care act was  that his greatest achievement  i don't think there's any question that  from a procedural point of view and from  moving a complex piece of legislation  through congress  the affordable care act is without  question harry reid's legacy  he believed that that bill would stand  the test of time not only politically  but practically and by all measures it  has more people are signing up yes it  requires subsidies but there is no  longer talk in republican circles as  there was vociferously for years about  repealing it and harry reid used every  tool in the book to deal with a 60-vote  senate majority of democrats remember  that to get democrats on board even  though they were reluctant and yes did  he hand out what were described at the  time probably accurately as special  favors in louisiana and nebraska to get  that 60-vote coalition yes he did but he  considered the goal worth all of the  hard work and  sometimes the uncomfortable deals  necessary to make that legislation not  only a reality but one that has stuck  and stayed in american political and  insurance life  indeed he will be long remembered uh  major garrett and joel payne always good  to see you both thank you very much
 

